{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/950519-game-scope","id":950519,"title":"Game scope","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-08-14T06:03:17-07:00","id":950519,"title":"Game scope","content":"Quick survey for Storytellers: When you run a Werewolf game, how to you frame the scope of your game?<br /> <br /> Do your players battle Methuselah, summon Incarna, join the Silver Pack, avert a handful of world-ending apocalypses, then take up arms and battle the Wyrm head on? Or is your game more of an episodic 'villain of the week' game, like Buffy the Vampire Slayer, but set in a Caern instead of a High School, and even if it's a world shaking event- it just so happens to be going down right at your players' doorstep.<br /> <br /> Is your game filled with Garou and Vampires and Demons (Oh My!) in a kaleidoscope of splats all running into each other, teaming up, breaking up, making up, and betraying one another daily like a WB tv series? Or do you typically play games centered on the Werewolf cosmology where other gamelines are like dark corners of the map marked &quot;Thar be dragons&quot; and any crossovers come with a whole storyline attached, or never happen at all?<br /> <br /> Do you usually play one-and-done stories, where the plot and &quot;victory condition&quot; is established from the start and you all play to a pre-defined conclusion? Or do you games usually start with a pile of character sheets and now you're trying to come up with something interesting for these people to do?<br /> <br /> Do you use fresh sheets every game? Let players dig into their binders and pull out a seasoned veteran? Or do you hand out a pile of extra points to get characters on par with the threat level of the story you had in mind? Or do you dare to hand out.... pre-made characters? (DUN Dun duuuuuuun)<br /> <br /> I'm curious to hear what the norm is, or how often your games fall into which category or where they fall in-between.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my mediocre WoD/Aeon/Exalted art at <a href=\"http://blackspinel.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://blackspinel.deviantart.com</a> ! It'll make you go &quot;Yup! That's pretty mediocre!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12162-crusher-of-cities","name":"Crusher-of-Cities","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12162_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-14T06:16:39-07:00","id":950526,"title":"","content":"I usually have the scope being on a more local level. Garou are incredibly rare, the world is incredibly messed up, and Garou are just too busy to take the eye off the ball for long.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-14T08:52:17-07:00","id":950577,"title":"","content":"Whew, lots of questions here. You ask whether our games are small or large scale, and my answer is... both. My game has been going on for a few years now; same characters, all of them scarred athro long-fangs. The players' pack has advanced to the point where they are fighting against fallen Incarna and taking on small armies of mockeries as caerns fall, the Storm Eater approaches, and the heads of the Triatic Wyrm stir. In the beginning, however, it was very small potatoes- rank 0 or rank 1 werewolves trying to find stolen horses for the local Iron Riders, or rites of passage that involved tracking and killing a single sneaky vampire that was tainting a village's water supply.<br /> <br /> WtA is a pretty rich game, but I feel like it loses some of the texture if you just start out feted for the Silver Pack and kicking the Maeljin Incarna's butts.<br /> <br /> I always go through and carefully discuss what sort of character the player has in mind. Something I have found myself doing in Werewolf more than in other games is delving into the family life of the Garou- who there parents were, who their mates are, how they relate to their children... often the answers aren't happy, but there are glimpses of what could have been<br /> <br /> Crossovers are fine- variety is the spice of life, after all, and having other supernaturals to murder is great fun for werewolves. The pack has killed vampires and will-workers, they have dealt with a confused spirit that *thinks* it is an angel, and they have had a few brushes with humans that act a lot like Imbued. Most of these have been either unwitting or outright servants of the Wyrm. The worldviews of the mages or the vampires are always skewed misinterpretations of the true nature of the cosmos, as these beings are not the chosen of Gaia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3695,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","user":"The Laughing Stranger","date":". 08-14-2016, 11:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-14T11:14:23-07:00","id":950654,"title":"","content":"I often try to keep it more low key/local for the first 2 ranks, then slowly expand it to where if they reach rank 4 (or heaven forbid 5) they are battling Majelin and incarna, such as the eater of souls...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-14T12:05:32-07:00","id":950681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/950519-game-scope?p=950654#post950654\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I often try to keep it more low key/local for the first 2 ranks, then slowly expand it to where if they reach rank 4 (or heaven forbid 5) they are battling Majelin and incarna, such as the eater of souls...</div> </div> </div> </div>Pretty much this for me as well, though I have yet to have a game where players reached rank 5. This is also my experience in games I've played in run by others. Rank 1 tends to be incredibly local. By rank 2 you start dealing with inter-Sept politics. Around rank 3 you start journeying across the region and further into the Umbra. Rank 4 characters are dealing with major issues that threaten the area and/or powerful Spirit beings. Rank 5 characters should be playing roles in shaping their Werewolf world.<br /> <br /> And since the OP covered so much territory.<br /> My worlds do have other supernaturals in them, but only in the numbers necessary for my stories. The actual specifics vary game to game. So in one game the Technocracy might exist and in another it doesn't. Whether or not the Technocracy is a thing doesn't matter for most Werewolf games.<br /> <br /> And I usually start a game out with a loose framework in mind, (a couple of stories) and let the game write itself from there. I've also run 1 shot games where reaching the goal ended the game and we moved onto something else. And if the group I'm playing with has enjoyed a 1 shot enough, then it can (and has) extended into a full game.<br /> <br /> I generally never use pre-made characters but have used them once for a series of intro games for the various World of Darkness lines (most of my current group had never played any WoD game). Even then the character sheets handed out were stereotype templates and the players got a handful of points to customize the characters better.<br /> Every other game though that I've ever played or ran the players write up their own characters, with as much help from the ST as they want to make their character make sense and fit into the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":805,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","user":"idpersona","date":". 08-14-2016, 03:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-14T17:19:13-07:00","id":950808,"title":"","content":"I set up a system of nearby caerns in the geographic area the game will take place in. I imagine what the forces of the Wyrm are like in this region. That is the sandbox of the game. The obvious things players will see.<br /> <br /> I then try to create a central mystery - what would be the plot I guess - in it. This is what the players don't see, at least not at first. However, I don't railroad. It's just another thing for the players to interact with if they want. But it is big enough in scope that I imagine &quot;solving&quot; it will be more than enough to last the entire chronicle. This is usually some horrible slumbering Wyrm creature. This is your Methusalehs fighting each other in Chicago, and your Seventh Generation and Bab Yaga in New York and Russia. Maybe it is that a Talon of the Wyrm is underneath the ground slumbering. Or that there is a Deep Umbral invasion coming to the region where bizarre things will keep arriving until someone goes on a high level spirit quest.<br /> <br /> Lastly are the features created by the players (individually and as a pack) themselves from their backgrounds, backstory, and their general and specific interests. Usually these are the hooks that I will feed them into areas of the sandbox I've already created.<br /> <br /> I do try to have the &quot;one big thing&quot; for each game session so that it always feels news and different - a reason to come to the game. That is either a new development in one of the things in the sandbox that's engaged them, something to do with the central mystery, or an event I've picked as flavor for the game (a Silent Strider arrives at the caern spreading news; a Lunatic is out killing people; a Ronin trespasses on their territory; a tribal moot occurs; etc.) However, sometimes the players just need to resolve other outstanding issues, and I've learned not to keep throwing new things at them.<br /> <br /> I generally over prepare my games. On the bad side, it is a lot of work for me, and sometimes it is too tough on the players who don't know where to go. But when it works, they have the freedom to choose to tell THEIR story, not mine, but they appreciate the depth of the setting. They know things aren't out there just for them, but there is a whole world going on.<br /> <br /> I want to see the characters and setting develop, so I am big on starting at rank one and working your way up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-14T22:08:59-07:00","id":950926,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>idpersona</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/950519-game-scope?p=950681#post950681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And since the OP covered so much territory.<br /> My worlds do have other supernaturals in them, but only in the numbers necessary for my stories. The actual specifics vary game to game. So in one game the Technocracy might exist and in another it doesn't. Whether or not the Technocracy is a thing doesn't matter for most Werewolf games.</div> </div> </div> </div>I forgot to comment on that part.<br /> For me, since i own the stuff for VtM and HtR, i put them supers in, but others are based on the NPC 'mages and such' from the main book for werewolf.. Not their own book versions..<br /> As to how many.. How many the game i plan for needs.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>idpersona</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/950519-game-scope?p=950681#post950681\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I generally never use pre-made characters but have used them once for a series of intro games for the various World of Darkness lines (most of my current group had never played any WoD game). Even then the character sheets handed out were stereotype templates and the players got a handful of points to customize the characters better.<br /> Every other game though that I've ever played or ran the players write up their own characters, with as much help from the ST as they want to make their character make sense and fit into the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've done pregen once, with a group of guys on ship to show them how easy it was to get into RPGs..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-15T11:43:08-07:00","id":951221,"title":"","content":"I tend to use crossovers a lot, so I will use multiple types of supernatural creatures in a single game, though the majority of them are going to be minor supernatural creatures (ghouls, kinain, kinfolk, possessed, sorcerers, etc). Vampires will tend to either be humane vampires that can avoid detection or inhuman vampires that set off every 'kil'l instinct in a werewolf. Mages are always going to be power hungry maniacs because that is what happens with every character in every Mage game that I have played in, as they devolve by the time that they reach Arete 4, so the difference between Technocracy and Traditions is merely academic in my Werewolf games (in fact, most Werewolves should not know the difference between the two, other than some Technocracy Mages smell more of the Weaver than some Tradition Mages). Wraiths tend to either be the Grateful Dead, using their powers to make their friends and family happy, or the Vengeful Dead, using their powers to make everyone alive miserable. Seelie Changelings are cute and quirky allies while Unseelie Changelings are pathetic and insane enemies. Demons are target practice.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-15T13:41:37-07:00","id":951287,"title":"","content":"I will say this: I don't like 'MtA' mages in my werewolf games. I dislike the postmodernism that forms the core of Ascension, and I think it squares badly with the clearly presented animistic world of Apocalypse.<br /> <br /> I tried using spheres, and did not care for the feel of them when juxtaposed with how the Garou operate, thematically speaking. In my game, will-workers, mages, sorcerers, they are all the same... humans who have stumbled upon some secret that allows them to exploit the weaker denizens of the spirit world (or conversely, be used by the spirits). Some are attuned to the Wyld, others slaves of the Weaver, and some are agents of the Wyrm, but all are messing with things they shouldn't. Many of them crave spiritual power and are blind to the disharmony they cause in the umbra. Some form wicked cabals intent on gaining the attention of whatever gods or demons they believe are behind their powers.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-16T05:23:30-07:00","id":951616,"title":"","content":"I limit games to themes that are local. If any plot has global repercussions, the players are supposed to send news to other septs, telling about their findings. But, unless a plot involves umbra travel, it usually takes place locally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}